  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Muggy with seasonably warm temperatures today.\nScattered showers arrive by the evening, continuing through \nFriday morning. Isolated moderate to heavy showers possible. \n\nToday a shortwave trough approaches from the northwest increasing \nrain chances toward the evening. While much of the day stays mostly \ndry, a plume of anomalous moisture advects in resulting in plentiful \nmoisture and humid/muggy day. PWAT values rise to around 2.0\" which \nis fairly anomalous (180-200% of normal). Across most of the CAMs, \nshowers begin to arrive after 2 PM in western MA/CT spreading \neastward this evening while becoming higher in coverage. There \ncontinues to be variance in the exact track of this shortwave, \nparticularly with how far north or south with respect to southern \nNew England. This will likely influence rain amounts and how \nwidespread the showers will be. A more north track will shift the \nstronger forcing to our north thus resulting in lighter rainfall \namounts overall. The 00Z HRRR, RRFS, and NAM (to some extent) have \nlean more south favoring higher rain amounts with the support of \nstronger ascent and marginal instability for isolated downpours. The \nsplit continues to bring uncertainty in the expected totals and the \npotential for some isolated downpours this evening and tonight. \nLeverage the full range of guidance, most areas should see between \n0.1\" to 0.6\". However, pockets of higher amounts of up to 1.0\" are \npossible within any embedded downpours. Mid-level flow should be \nstrong enough to keep the showers moving which will reduce the flash \nflood threat. Additionally the latest HREF doesn't highlight any \npotential areas for potential to exceed 1hr and 3hr FFG.\n\nCold front moves through early Friday morning with winds shifting to \nthe NW behind it. Showers ahead of the front push across the \nCape/Islands, exiting by mid-morning. Breezy NW winds in the \nafternoon with gusts 20-25 mph, mainly across the interior.\nTemperatures are likely to be in seasonable ranges in the 70s. A \ncooler than normal airmass settles across the region by Saturday \nbringing Fall-like temperatures in the 60s. Surface high pressure \ndominates bringing dry conditions. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...- Next chance for rain Sunday into Monday. There\nremains uncertainty in the details such as coverage, timing, \namounts. Showers may linger through early next week.\n\nThe main surface high exits offshore Saturday night into early \nSunday as the next system approaches from the west with our next \nchance of precipitation. Shower/rain chances increase Sunday morning \ninto Sunday afternoon from west to east. Plentiful moisture advects \nin within the southerly flow to support showers as the warm sector \npushes northward into southern New England. PWATs increase to 1.0-\n1.8\" with the higher values across CT and RI. Soundings show deep \nmoisture profiles further supporting efficient rainfall. Rain likely \ncontinues into early Monday morning. There is some question in if a \nfrontal boundary stalls to the south which may result in lingering \nshowers in areas Monday (south coast, Cape/Islands). \n\nThe potential rain amounts will depend on the exact track of \nthe system which is still variable across guidance this far out \nin time. This variance translates quite clearly to the rain \ntotals across both deterministic and ensemble guidance. \nEnsembles range generally between 0.70 to upwards of 2.0\". The \nGFS and Canadian ensembles have leaned on the lower end of that \nrange(even lower than 0.70\" in areas) compared to the ECMWF \nensemble. On the other hand the AI- ECMWF ens and AI-GEFS have \nless than 0.60\" QPF with similar solutions in the AI \ndeterministic runs. A decent amount of spread still exists in \nhow much rain will fall Sunday into Monday, but overall looks \nlike a good shot for a soaking rain. Depending on if and where a\nstationary boundary sets up nearby, showers may linger through \nthe early part of the week.",
